><text color="#9643FC" creationdate="D:20250708112239+10'00'" flags="print,nozoom,norotate" inreplyto="55d1b2b2-7e5e-482b-a7a1-f02e796d3ca9" date="D:20250708112239+10'00'" name="6e020a9a-04f2-499c-ae96-16ad1044dd20" icon="Comment" page="10" rect="50.552032,275.691681,74.552032,299.691681" subject="Sticky Note" title="Alexander Gibson"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>It would appear prudent to develop palatable&#xD;athlete-centered (and practitioner) resources to improve their&#xD;knowledge and beliefs/attitudes regarding training. Such&#xD;upskilling would provide athletes with evidence to inform&#xD;their training modifications in response to germane situations&#xD;(e.g., COVID-related situations, injury, and illness).&#xD;Sports organizations or teams should provide necessary&#xD;resources to athletes, regardless of their classifications, by&#xD;utilizing online learning and interaction platforms that offer&#xD;free access to seminars and workshops. In this context, a&#xD;specific approach to information delivery is required to target&#xD;athletes across different classifications. The data suggest&#xD;that stakeholders would benefit from policy and resources&#xD;(including support) to facilitate remote training with their&#xD;athletes.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="10" rect="595.276001,207.691681,779.276001,299.691681"
/></text
></annots
><f href="10.1007_s40279-021-01573-z.pdf"
/><ids original="12F3660BDA04644F889FB6D1B79DB155" modified="6E46D24E857BD149A76CA7F802ABB298"
/></xfdf
>